message.content:
{
  "summary": "This bill would require all public schools to provide child trafficking prevention and awareness education as part of health education for students in all grades from kindergarten through 12th grade. Child trafficking is defined as a human trafficking offense where the victim is under 18 years old. Local education agencies (LEAs) and public charter schools must ensure this instruction is age- and grade-appropriate and based on health education standards adopted by the state board of education. These schools may use qualified social workers or healthcare professionals to help teach this material, provided those individuals meet specific requirements for teaching family life. 

By August 1, 2026, and every August 1 after that, each public charter school governing body and local board of education must publish a plan on their website and submit it to the commissioner of education. This plan must describe the instruction methods for each grade, the qualifications of the instructors, and the resources used. Additionally, by July 1, 2027, and every July 1 after that, these bodies must submit a report to the commissioner of education verifying that the required instruction was provided during the previous school year and providing the same details on methods, qualifications, and resources. 

The bill also amends Tennessee Code Annotated Section 49-1-1002(a) to include child trafficking prevention and awareness education and deletes specific language from Tennessee Code Annotated Section 49-6-1304. This act would take effect upon becoming law and applies to the 2026-2027 school year and following years."
}
